## 04.1 Critical Distinction

A timestamp generator is not automatically a valid forced aligner.

A word-level timestamp is not automatically an accurate word boundary.

A model that produces one timestamp per token may not distinguish the full acoustic duration of a word.

You must evaluate onset and offset separately.

Required measurements:

```text
WORD_COVERAGE
START_BOUNDARY_ERROR_MS
END_BOUNDARY_ERROR_MS
MEDIAN_BOUNDARY_ERROR_MS
P90_BOUNDARY_ERROR_MS
P95_BOUNDARY_ERROR_MS
INVALID_INTERVAL_RATE
MISSING_WORD_RATE
```

Preserve repeated words as independent events.

Do not combine two repeated words merely because their text is identical.

## 04.2 Modern Alignment Evaluation

Investigate the FA-Bench methodology:

https://github.com/olewave/fa-bench

Research paper:

https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32396

This work highlights an important evaluation issue: ordinary MAE may overstate the quality of recognition-dependent alignment because incorrect and missing transcript words can be overlooked.

Implement or adapt a dual-track evaluation:

**Track A — Reference Transcript Alignment**

Provide the correct manually verified transcript to the aligner.

This measures the alignment system independently of ASR errors.

**Track B — Recognition-Driven Alignment**

Provide the actual ASR-generated transcript to the alignment system.

This measures the complete product pipeline.

The second track must penalize:

- Recognized words that were inserted.
- Words deleted by ASR.
- Words that cannot be aligned.
- Incorrect word labels.
- Invalid neighboring boundaries.

Use MAE and percentile errors, but also evaluate boundary precision/recall or a tolerance-based F1 metric where methodologically appropriate.

Predefine tolerances before testing.

Never select evaluation thresholds after seeing candidate results.

